        "eyebrow": "Virtual staging near you",
        "heading": "Virtual Staging Toronto, ON",
        "lede": "Virtual staging is a digital marketing technique for real estate photos: an interior designer adds 3D furniture and decor to empty or cleared rooms so potential buyers can see how the space lives, with no physical furniture ever entering the home. Bella's designers stage Toronto listings in 24 to 48 hours, from {price:virtual-staging} a photo.",
        "cta_label": "Stage my listing — from {price:virtual-staging}",
        "cta_link": "/products/virtual-staging",
        "alt_label": "Browse the lookbook",
        "alt_link": "/pages/lookbook",
        "city": "Toronto",
        "market_heading": "Why virtual staging matters in the Toronto real estate market",
        "market_text": "<p>Toronto is a condo-heavy market, and an empty 500 sq ft unit in a King West tower photographs like every other empty unit in the building. When a buyer is scrolling through dozens of near-identical floor plans, staged photos are how one listing stands out and gets the click, the saved search and the showing request.</p><p>Small-space proof is the specific job here. In one-bedroom and junior one-bedroom downtown condos, the buyer's real question is whether a bed, a sofa and a work-from-home desk can coexist. Furniture placement drawn to scale answers it in the photo; empty rooms leave the buyer assuming the answer is no.</p><p>Toronto's freehold market has the opposite problem. Narrow Victorian semis and century homes have room proportions and additions that bare-wall photography makes confusing, and a vacant space gives potential buyers nothing to judge scale or layout against. Virtually staged photos give them somewhere to put themselves, which is why empty rooms are the first thing we fix when an agent needs to sell.</p><p>Pre-construction and newly completed inventory is the third case. Builders and investors listing finished suites that have never been lived in can present every unit in the best light without renting and moving physical furniture between floors.</p>",
        "local_heading": "Toronto virtual staging for condos, freeholds and pre-construction",
        "local_text": "<p>Designers stage clean contemporary for King West, Liberty Village and downtown condos, warm transitional for Leslieville, Roncesvalles and the Danforth, and family looks for North York, Etobicoke and the 905. Design styles are chosen per room, and our designers match the furniture, decor and accessories to the buyer each property attracts, with everything scaled honestly so nothing surprises anyone at the showing.</p><p>The virtual staging process is simple: order online, upload photos from your photo shoot, and high-resolution, MLS-ready images come back in 24 to 48 hours for TRREB and Realtor.ca, with disclosure guidance included. TRREB permits virtual staging as long as the edits don't materially alter the image's factual accuracy or misrepresent any feature, condition or view of the property, so we add furniture and decor only. Our <a href=\"/pages/resources-mls-rules-canada\">Canadian virtual staging compliance guide</a> covers labelling on the MLS and Realtor.ca.</p><p>Volume discounts apply automatically: 5% at 3+ photos, 10% at 6+, 15% at 11+ and 20% at 21+.</p>",
        "hoods_label": "Where we stage most",
        "hoods": "King West, Liberty Village, Leslieville, Roncesvalles, The Danforth, Yorkville, Riverdale, North York, Etobicoke, Scarborough",
        "cred_text": ""
      }
