        "q2": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How much does it cost to stage a house in Toronto?",
            "answer": "Physical staging is priced by the stager, the size of the home and how long the furniture stays, so quotes vary; as a general benchmark, conventional staging runs about $2,000–5,000 a month until the home sells. Virtual staging is {price:virtual-staging} a photo, paid once, with no rental period to extend if the listing sits."
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is virtual staging allowed on TRREB listings?",
            "answer": "Yes. TRREB permits virtual staging provided the edits don't materially alter the factual accuracy of the image or misrepresent any feature, condition or view of the property. Add furniture and decor only, label the staged photos, and keep the original photo in the listing so buyers can compare. Our Canadian compliance guide covers the details."
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How can I virtually stage my home for free?",
            "answer": "Free AI apps exist, but they tend to invent details and misjudge scale, which is risky on an MLS listing. The lowest-risk way to try designer staging is the $40 voucher new clients can claim on Bella's sign-up page — eligibility is subject to Bella's approval."
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Which virtual staging companies are considered the best?",
            "answer": "Look for three things: a real designer composing each room rather than an AI template, furniture drawn to the room's true scale and lighting, and a clear revision policy. Bella offers unlimited revisions for two weeks and reworks or refunds any image that still isn't right."
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Every condo in this building looks identical. Can staging actually differentiate mine?",
            "answer": "That's the strongest case for it in Toronto. When a buyer has scrolled a dozen empty versions of the same floor plan, the staged one is the listing that reads as somewhere to live rather than a plan number."
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "It's a 480 sq ft junior one-bedroom. Is staging going to look ridiculous?",
            "answer": "Not if it's done to scale, which is the point. The buyer wants to know whether a real bed and a real sofa fit — furniture that oversizes the room fails that test at the showing, so we furnish honestly."
          }
        },
        "q8": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How does the virtual staging process work?",
            "answer": "Order online, then upload your listing photos — right after checkout or later. A designer clears the room if needed, stages it in your chosen style and delivers MLS-ready images in 24 to 48 hours. Mark up any changes and download the final files."
          }
        },
        "q9": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I use the staged images anywhere besides MLS?",
            "answer": "Yes — you keep full usage rights. MLS, Realtor.ca, a virtual tour, social ads, brochures and print all work, provided the virtual staging is disclosed in each."
          }
        },
        "q10": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Which Toronto properties benefit most from virtual staging?",
            "answer": "Vacant condos first — an empty space gives potential buyers nothing to anchor to. After that: small or awkward layouts where furniture placement proves the room works, tenanted units where we remove the existing furniture, completed pre-construction inventory, and stale listings that need a reason to relaunch."
          }
        },
